# Geppetto build target: dgx-spark
#
# aarch64 + NVIDIA GB10 Grace Blackwell (sm_121): DGX Spark and equivalents.
#
# STATUS: VERIFIED end to end on a DGX Spark (Ubuntu 24.04, driver 580.173.02).
#   torch 2.13.0+cu130 · xformers 0.0.35 · all six CUDA extensions compiled for
#   sm_121 · 512³ in 171 s, 1024³ in 294 s, both producing valid meshes.
#
# Three risks were identified before the hardware was available. For the record,
# what actually happened:
#
#  1. sm_121 kernels — NOT a problem. Stock wheels were reported to compile only
#     to sm_120, leaving GB10 on PTX JIT, with diffusion sampling hanging
#     indefinitely. torch 2.13.0+cu130 runs it without incident.
#  2. Sparse attention — NOT a problem, but it remains the load-bearing
#     dependency. trellis2/modules/sparse/config.py accepts only xformers /
#     flash_attn / flash_attn_3 — no sdpa, no naive. There is no flash-attn
#     wheel for aarch64+sm_121, so this target relies on xformers, which builds
#     from source cleanly. If that ever breaks, this target cannot run at all.
#  3. The CUDA extensions — NOT a problem. nvdiffrast, nvdiffrec, cumesh,
#     flexgemm and o-voxel all compile against CUDA 13 on arm64.
#
# Expect roughly 1.4–2.5× the workstation's times: GB10's unified memory runs
# ~273 GB/s against an RTX 6000 Ada's ~960 GB/s, and this workload is
# bandwidth-bound. The Spark's advantage is 121 GB of unified memory on a
# dedicated always-on box, not raw speed. VRAM is a non-issue — peak measured
# there is 4.1 GB at 1024³.
BASE_IMAGE=nvidia/cuda:13.0.0-devel-ubuntu24.04
CUDA_ARCH=12.1
PYTHON_VERSION=3.12
TORCH_SPEC=torch torchvision
TORCH_INDEX_URL=https://download.pytorch.org/whl/cu130
ATTN_PACKAGE=xformers
ATTN_PACKAGE_FALLBACK=xformers
ATTN_IMPORT=xformers
DEFAULT_ATTN_BACKEND=xformers
GEPPETTO_TARGET=dgx-spark
